<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tribal Hidage</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tribal Hidage is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> list of 35 peoples south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, giving the number of hides, units of land assessment, attributed to each. It probably records tribute owed to a Mercian or possibly Northumbrian overlord and is usually dated between the 7th and 9th centuries; the oldest copy is an 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>. Many of the small groups it names, such as the Elmetsæte, are otherwise almost unknown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tribal-hidage.jpg" alt="Tribal Hidage" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Equestenebrarum; derivative work by Amitchell125 at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tribal_Hidage_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Three versions, or recensions, of the list survive, all apparently copied from one lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>. Recension A, the earliest and most complete, was written by several scribes no later than 1032 in a miscellany of Old English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works that includes Aelfric's Latin Grammar, now in the British Library. Recension B appears in a Latin treatise written by Henry Spelman in 1626, and Recension C survives in six Latin documents, four of 13th-century origin and two a century older.</p><p>Historians disagree on when and where the original was compiled. Most hold that it was Mercian, since Mercia heads the list and its kings held power over the peoples south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> from the late 7th to the early 9th century; Wendy Davies and Hayo Vierck placed it at 670-690. Nicholas Brooks argued for a Northumbrian origin, which would explain the inclusion of Elmet and the absence of Deira and Bernicia, and N. J. Higham linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a> in the 620s, a theory D. P. Kirby says has not been generally accepted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The list opens with Mercia at 30,000 hides, followed by small peoples to its west and north assessed at no more than 7,000 hides each, among them the Wrekin dwellers, the Lindsey folk and the Western men. Others have between 300 and 1,200 hides; some of these, such as the Herefinna, Noxgaga, Hendrica and Unecungaga, cannot be identified, and others, such as the Hicca, have been located only through place-names.</p><p>The list ends with the larger kingdoms: the East Angles at 30,000 hides, the East Saxons at 7,000, Kent at 15,000, the South Saxons at 7,000 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> at 100,000. The round figures make an accurate survey unlikely. Cyril Hart saw it as a tribute list made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>, P. H. Sawyer as a symbolic expression of each kingdom's status, and Frank Stenton judged the figures for Mercia and Wessex at variance with other information.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Spelman first published the text, but discussion began only with John Mitchell Kemble's The Saxons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1848. Walter de Gray Birch announced the discovery of Recension A in 1884, and Frederic William Maitland gave the list its name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> and Beyond in 1897. In 1971 Hart mapped southern England as Mercia's provinces and dependencies. The list is generally agreed to be the earliest fiscal document to survive from medieval England, and it records the kingdom of East Anglia for the first time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tribal Hidage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A document known as the <b>Tribal Hidage</b>, probably drawn up for a Mercian overlord in the 7th or 8th century, assesses the peoples of southern England in hides, the units of land that supported a household and owed dues to the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The district appears in the <b>Tribal Hidage</b> as the Elmetsæte, and its people were absorbed into Northumbria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-elmet">Kingdom of Elmet</a></li><li><blockquote>The many small peoples of the region, listed in the <b>Tribal Hidage</b>, kept their own identities.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-middle-angles">Kingdom of the Middle Angles</a></li></ul></section>
